itive", "Raters split 7 Tossup, 2 Lean R, 1 Likely D, and outside groups have spent $830K attacking Wittman"], "rationale": "A favorable Democratic climate and the highest-quality recent poll give Taylor a small edge, but Wittman's incumbency, the district's Republican lean, and his large financial advantage keep the race near even; markets and most raters land in the same narrow band.", "watch": "Additional late polling or a Wittman advertising surge funded by his cash advantage could move the race either way in the final two weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 1st District is one of the closest House races of 2026. Republican Rob Wittman, seeking another term in a district that leans Republican by three points, faces Democrat Shannon Taylor, a well-funded former prosecutor. Seven of ten raters call the race a Tossup, with two rating it Lean Republican and one rating it Likely Democratic.\n\nRecent polling is thin but tilts toward Taylor: a Washington Post-Schar School survey of likely voters in late September put her ahead 50-46, while a Democratic committee poll a week earlier had Wittman up one. Prediction markets give Taylor roughly a 52-55 percent chance. Wittman holds a large financial edge, with $4.3 million on hand to Taylor's $1.0 million, though outside groups have spent about $830,000 attacking him. A strong Democratic national environment keeps this race on a knife's edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4341, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4371, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3736, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3951, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4096, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4532, "margi